h description noting it's a continuation.\n\nSince this is a fragment, I should extract:\n\n- certificate metadata (number, revision, project number, member state, page_total=11)\n- matrix_tables for the tables present\n- model_family models (DN sizes)\n- characteristics model_level\n\nThe schema says to return a JSON object conforming to the layered schema \"for the content present in THIS fragment only\". So I should include:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"revision\": 5?,\n    \"project_number\": \"2496280\",\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"page_total\": 11\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, revision — the certificate number is \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 5\". The schema has revision_history[] but that's for revision history entries. The \"revision 5\" here is the certificate revision. I could put it in certificate as \"revision\": \"5\" maybe. The schema doesn't have a certificate.revision field explicitly but \"Don't fabricate\" — but this is present. I'll add it as a field. Actually the schema shows certificate fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. The number includes \"revision 5\". I could set number to \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add \"revision\": \"5\". Since rule 8 says omit fields not present, but adding a revision field seems reasonable since the data is present. Let me include it as \"revision\": \"5\" (string like revision_history convention). Actually revision_history revision is string per schema. I'll do \"revision\": \"5\".\n\nScheme: \"A\" — the number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 contains \"-A-\" which indicates scheme A. The instruction says scheme (A|B). It's derivable from the number. I'll include \"scheme\": \"A\". Hmm, but is it safe? OIML-CS certificates: \"A\" in the number indicates OIML certificate scheme A. The recommendation scheme... Actually \"scheme\" under certificate refers to the OIML CS scheme (A or B). The n