l_level: flow attributes for Table 5 models:\n     - minimum_flow_q1 (m³/h)\n     - transitional_flow_q2 (m³/h)\n     - permanent_flow_q3 (m³/h)\n     - overload_flow_q4 (m³/h)\n     - ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n     - accuracy_class? That's an attribute of the model, put in model attributes.\n   \n   Should the notes be captured? \"Values Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values, ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25; ratio Q3/Q1 at least 40.\" These are footnotes/notes. I could add to matrix_tables.footnotes or a description. I'll add to the Table 5 matrix_table description and/or footnotes. The note repeats after both table parts. I'll add a \"notes\" in description.\n\nAlso for indicating device tables: model_level entries? The attributes: indicating_range_max (m³), indicating_range_min (m³), verification_scale_interval_max (m³), varying by size-group × config. I'll keep them in matrix_tables only.\n\nShould ratio Q2/Q1=1.6 and Q4/Q3=1.25 be config/type-level characteristics? They're constraints, not values per se. I'll put them in matrix_tables description or as type_level characteristics:\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 (Q1, Q2 minimum values constraint)\n- ratio_q4_q3: 1.25\n- min ratio q3/q1: 40.\n\nMaybe add as type_level characteristics:\n- \"q2_over_q1_ratio\": {value: 1.6}\n- \"q4_over_q3_ratio\": {value: 1.25}\n- \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {value: 40}\n\nHmm, these are rule constraints from a note. I could include them in type_level. It's reasonable. But rule: characteristics.type_level = whole-cert attributes. These apply to the whole family. I'll include them as type_level with clear labels: \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"ratio_q4_q3\", \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\". Actually careful with rule 1: canonical labels, no punctuation. Fine.\n\nAlso certificate: number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", revision \"5\", project_number \"2496280\", page_total 11, member_state \"The Netherlands\", scheme \"A\".\n\nrecommendation: R49 (water meters), edition 2013. The number \"R